# run_e_plus_5_kernel.g — A-5b driver: compute and persist ker(Tr) for E_+(5).
#
# Implements issue #47 ("A-5b E_+(5) Tier-4 end-to-end driver").
#
# Goal: produce ker Tr(E_+(5)) as an explicit subgroup of (Z/torsion[1]) x ... x (Z/torsion[k]).
#
#   G = E_+(5) = ExtraspecialGroup(2048, "+"). |G| = 2^11 = 2048. NOT
#   in SmallGroup library (which stops at order 2000). Inside this
#   driver we use G = conj_action.em = Image(IsomorphismPcGroup(EmGroup(5)))
#   — a pc-group of order 2048, isomorphic to ExtraspecialGroup(2048, "+").
#   H^3(G; U(1)) torsion: DISCOVERED AT RUNTIME via SetupCohomology3.
#   The torsion is printed; do not hardcode m=3-shaped assumptions.
#
# Brute-force enumeration is infeasible. We use:
#
#   * T-3d BRW orbit reduction: conjugacy classes collapse to BRW orbits
#     under O^+(2m, F_2) acting on E_+(m); the per-generator transgression
#     check on 4 orbit reps suffices, then results are pulled back to all
#     all classes via the H^3-action matrices.
#   * T-4 centraliser-cache (~4 iso-types of centralisers across the 65
#     classes) so the heavy ResolutionFiniteGroup is done once per type
#     inside `BuildPerClassCocycleBasis`.
#   * T-5 resumable per-generator persistence: each completed generator is
#     appended to a state file, so crashes lose at most one generator's
#     wall-clock.
#   * Solver dispatch (`IsCoboundary2Split`): order-2 generators take the
#     mod-2 + Bockstein-image fast path; the order-4 generator takes the
#     iterated-Bockstein Z/2^k path (k discovered at runtime).
#
# The per-generator sweep alone gives us, by linearity of Tr, the SUBKERNEL
# generated by individual e_i that lie in ker Tr. To capture the full
# kernel as a subgroup of (Z/torsion[1]) x ... x (Z/torsion[k]) we additionally need to test:
#
#   * For each i in [1..k]: is e_i in ker?
#   * For the highest-torsion generator (index exp_index): is the half-vector in ker?
#
# By Tr being a Z-linear homomorphism, the full kernel is the subgroup of
# Z/2^34 (+) Z/4 generated by these tested basis-and-half vectors (those
# answering "yes"). Mixed sums like (e_i + 2 e_35) for i <= 34 are in ker
# iff both e_i and 2 e_35 are, by linearity. So this set of (at most) 36
# tests (35 unit basis + the half vector 2 e_35) characterises the kernel
# completely.
#
# Output format: similar to ../../computed/transgression/2/kernel.g but
# kernel_vecs is only enumerated when |ker| <= 2^16 (= 65536). For E_+(5)
# the kernel is may be large, so we persist it STRUCTURALLY as
# basis_summary + kernel_size + in_kernel_gens; kernel_vecs is `fail` in
# that case. Downstream consumers reconstruct elements via
# `Sum([1..Length(basis_summary)], t -> coeffs[t] * basis_summary[t].vec)
#  mod torsion`.
#
# Wall budget at m=5 unknown a priori; expected several minutes per orbit-rep
# cache (one per BRW orbit rep). The number of BRW orbits
# and per-orbit centraliser sizes are printed at runtime. After that, each of the k generator sweeps + the
# half-vec check is a pure matrix-vector + IsCoboundary2Split affair on
# the cached basis tables — sub-second per generator. Total: well within
# the 1-hour budget.
#
# Usage:
#
#   gap -q -b -c 'Read("run_e_plus_5_kernel.g");'
#
# from extraspecial_orbifolds/scripts/.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Setup: load the BRW per-generator path + resumable wrapper.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

driver_orig_cwd := DirectoryCurrent();;

# transgression_per_generator_brw.g pulls in transgression_kernel.g (UCT
# setup) and snf_wrapper.g (cocycle-basis cache + dispatcher). Both rely
# on CWD = scripts/ at Read-time.
Read("transgression_per_generator_brw.g");
# transgression_resume.g defines PersistGenerator / LoadResumeState /
# TransgressionKernelResumable. Lives in scripts/.
Read("transgression_resume.g");

# Restore CWD (Read() above may have chdir'd inside its shim chain).
ChangeDirectoryCurrent(Filename(driver_orig_cwd, ""));;

t_start := Runtime();;

Print("\n=== A-5b driver: ker(Tr) for E_+(5) (order 2048) ===\n");

# IMPORTANT: the BRW-amortised per-generator path requires G to be the
# matrix-group EmGroup(m). We DEFER the G binding to come from
# conj_action.em below, so that ConjugacyClasses(G) numbering matches
# BRWConjugacyAction(m)'s perm action exactly (otherwise we'd be using
# a different group instance and class numbering could disagree).

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 1: amortised per-generator BRW sweep with resumable state.
#
# IMPORTANT optimisation vs TransgressionKernelResumable(G, ., true):
# the off-the-shelf wrapper calls PerGeneratorTransgressionBRW(G, i)
# once per generator, which rebuilds the BRW conjugacy + H^3 actions
# AND the per-orbit cocycle basis caches from scratch each time. For
# E_+(5) with k generators and a small number of BRW orbits whose centralisers
# include the larger centralisers (n*n*k HAP cocycle evals per cache
# build) would multiply k× per generator and blow the 1-hour budget.
#
# Here we build the BRW data + 4 orbit-rep caches ONCE, then loop over
# generators replaying just the cheap matrix-vector + IsCoboundary2Split
# steps per generator. State persistence remains crash-safe via
# PersistGenerator from T-5.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

Print("\n--- Step 1: amortised BRW sweep (resumable) ---\n");

# State file: sweep_state.g in the output dir. Crash-safe append-only.
OutDir := "../../computed/transgression/5";;
Exec(Concatenation("mkdir -p ", OutDir));
StateFile := Concatenation(OutDir, "/sweep_state.g");;
Print("State file: ", StateFile, "\n");

# Build BRW conjugacy + H^3 actions once. h3_action internally builds the
# H^3 setup record (resolution + UCT) for us, so we share it and avoid a
# duplicate SetupCohomology3 call. We pick our G as conj_action.em so the
# class numbering for ConjugacyClasses(G) matches conj_action.perms.
m := 5;;
Print("Building BRW conjugacy + H^3 actions for m = ", m,
      " (this includes HAP setup for E_+(5))...\n");
t_brw := Runtime();;
conj_action := BRWConjugacyAction(m);;
h3_action := BRWH3Action(m);;
Print("BRW actions wall: ", (Runtime() - t_brw) / 1000., " s\n");

# G == conj_action.em ensures that conj_action.classes, perms,
# Centralizer(G, rep) etc. all line up. h3_action.em is a separate
# EmGroup(m) call but yields an equal group (same elements). We use
# h3_action.setup throughout for SetupCohomology3 data; this is the
# same data PerGeneratorTransgressionBRW would build for G.
G := conj_action.em;;
Assert(0, Order(G) = 2048,
       Concatenation("EmGroup(5) has |G| = ", String(Order(G)),
                     ", expected 2048 (= 2^11)"));
Print("|G| = ", Order(G), "\n");

setup := h3_action.setup;;
torsion := setup.torsion;;
exp := setup.exponent;;
k := setup.num_generators;;
Print("torsion = ", torsion, "\n");
Print("exponent = ", exp, "\n");
Print("k = ", k, " generators\n");

# Anchor: at m=5 we don't yet have a Quillen-derived torsion prediction
# (Q-int A2 #136 in progress). Just record what HAP discovers; the runtime
# torsion vector becomes the empirical anchor for downstream work (D-VAN-7,
# A-5d). Sanity-check minimum structure: at least one generator and
# all torsion entries are powers of 2.
Assert(0, k >= 1,
       Concatenation("k must be positive; got k = ", String(k)));
sorted_torsion := ShallowCopy(torsion);;
Sort(sorted_torsion);;
Assert(0, ForAll(torsion, t -> IsInt(t) and t >= 2 and IsPrimePowerInt(t)
                              and SmallestRootInt(t) = 2),
       Concatenation("expected all torsion entries to be 2-powers; got ",
                     String(torsion)));
Print("torsion shape sorted = ", sorted_torsion, "\n");

perm_gens := conj_action.perms;;
h3_matrices := h3_action.matrices;;
Assert(0, Length(perm_gens) = Length(h3_matrices),
       "perm_gens / h3_matrices length mismatch");

# IMPORTANT: use conj_action.classes (not ConjugacyClasses(G)), since
# the perms in conj_action.perms are indexed into conj_action.classes
# and using a re-computed ConjugacyClasses(G) would risk a numbering
# mismatch.
classes := conj_action.classes;;
n_classes := Length(classes);;
Print("n_classes = ", n_classes, " (expected ~150-300 at m=5; ",
      "no hardcoded value)\n");
Assert(0, n_classes >= 1,
       Concatenation("expected at least 1 conjugacy class; got ",
                     String(n_classes)));

# BFS-traverse class action under BRW perm_gens to identify orbits and
# Schreier vectors. We'll iterate orbit start indices in [1..n_classes]
# in increasing order, mirroring PerGeneratorTransgressionBRW.
Print("Identifying BRW orbits + Schreier vectors over ", n_classes,
      " classes...\n");
visited_all := BlistList([1..n_classes], []);;
orbits_data := [];;       # each entry: rec(start_idx, orbit, parent, gen_used)
for start_idx in [1..n_classes] do
    if visited_all[start_idx] then continue; fi;
    sv := OrbitWithSchreierVec(perm_gens, n_classes, start_idx);
    for target_idx in sv.orbit do
        visited_all[target_idx] := true;
    od;
    Add(orbits_data, rec(start_idx := start_idx,
                          orbit := sv.orbit,
                          parent := sv.parent,
                          gen_used := sv.gen_used));
od;
Print("Found ", Length(orbits_data), " BRW orbits.\n");
for orb_info in orbits_data do
    Print("  orbit at class ", orb_info.start_idx, ": ",
          Length(orb_info.orbit),
          " members, types = ", Set(conj_action.types{orb_info.orbit}),
          ", |C_G(rep)| = ",
          Order(Centralizer(G,
                            Representative(classes[orb_info.start_idx]))), "\n");
od;

# Build per-orbit cocycle-basis caches (one per orbit rep). This is the
# expensive step (HAP cocycle evals). At m=5 the orbit count is printed at runtime.
#
# Optimisation: for the identity-class orbit (rep = identity), c_g(h, k)
# = omega(id, h, k) - omega(h, id, k) + omega(h, k, id) = 0 for any
# normalised omega. We can detect this case and use a degenerate cache
# (table = all zeros) instead of running the n*n*k HAP evals — this skips
# the most expensive cache build (|C| = 128 for E_+(5)).
Print("Building per-orbit cocycle-basis caches...\n");
t_caches := Runtime();;
orbit_caches := [];;       # parallel to orbits_data
for orb_info in orbits_data do
    rep := Representative(classes[orb_info.start_idx]);
    C := Centralizer(G, rep);
    Print("  orbit start ", orb_info.start_idx, " (|C| = ", Order(C),
          ", n*n*k = ", Order(C)^2 * k,
          ", rep order = ", Order(rep), ") ... ");
    t_one := Runtime();
    if IsOne(rep) then
        # Identity rep: c_g ≡ 0. Build a degenerate cache with table of
        # all zeros (avoiding the HAP cocycle evals). We still need the
        # element list / index map for MakeCgFromCache to work.
        cache_elts := AsList(C);
        cache_n := Length(cache_elts);
        cache_table := List([1..cache_n * cache_n],
                            r -> List([1..k], i -> 0));
        cache_idx_map := NewDictionary(cache_elts[1], true, cache_elts);
        for cache_a in [1..cache_n] do
            AddDictionary(cache_idx_map, cache_elts[cache_a], cache_a);
        od;
        Add(orbit_caches, rec(C := C, table := cache_table,
                               elts := cache_elts, n := cache_n,
                               idx_map := cache_idx_map));
        Print((Runtime() - t_one) / 1000., " s (degenerate, identity class)\n");
    else
        Add(orbit_caches, BuildPerClassCocycleBasis(setup, rep, C));
        Print((Runtime() - t_one) / 1000., " s\n");
    fi;
od;
Print("Per-orbit caches wall: ", (Runtime() - t_caches) / 1000., " s total\n");

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-generator sweep using shared caches.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

completed_idx := Set(LoadResumeState(StateFile), r -> r.generator);;
Print("\nAlready completed (resume): ", Length(completed_idx), " / ", k,
      "  ", completed_idx, "\n");

# Helper: run the BRW-amortised transgression check for a single vec
# (arbitrary coefficient vector, not necessarily a unit). Uses the
# precomputed orbit_caches; iterates over orbits_data; returns the
# per-class boolean list.
PerVecTransgressionShared := function(vec)
    local results, oi, orb_info, basis_cache, target_idx, vec_target,
          c_g_table, c_g_func;
    results := List([1..n_classes], j -> "?");
    for oi in [1..Length(orbits_data)] do
        orb_info := orbits_data[oi];
        basis_cache := orbit_caches[oi];
        for target_idx in orb_info.orbit do
            vec_target := H3VecAtTarget(orb_info.start_idx, target_idx,
                                         orb_info.parent, orb_info.gen_used,
                                         h3_matrices, torsion, vec);
            c_g_table := List(basis_cache.table,
                              row -> Sum([1..k], idx ->
                                  vec_target[idx] * row[idx]));
            c_g_func := MakeCgFromCache(c_g_table, basis_cache);
            results[target_idx] :=
                IsCoboundary2Split(basis_cache.C, c_g_func, vec_target,
                                   setup);
        od;
    od;
    return results;
end;;

# Sweep loop.
t_sweep := Runtime();;
for i in [1..k] do
    if i in completed_idx then
        Print("Skipping generator ", i, " (already done)\n");
        continue;
    fi;
    t_gen := Runtime();
    e_i := List([1..k], j -> 0);
    e_i[i] := 1;
    per_class := PerVecTransgressionShared(e_i);
    all_in_kernel := ForAll(per_class, x -> x = true);
    PersistGenerator(StateFile, i, all_in_kernel, per_class);
    Print("Computing generator ", i, "... ",
          If_(all_in_kernel, "IN", "NOT"), " kernel  (",
          (Runtime() - t_gen) / 1000., " s)\n");
od;
sweep_wall := (Runtime() - t_sweep) / 1000.;;
Print("Per-generator sweep wall (this invocation): ", sweep_wall, " s\n");

# Re-load state to project to kernel_gen_indices (handles the resumable case).
final_state := LoadResumeState(StateFile);;
in_kernel_gens := List(Filtered(final_state, r -> r.all_in_kernel),
                        r -> r.generator);;
Print("In-kernel unit generators: ", Length(in_kernel_gens), " / ", k, "\n");
Print("  indices: ", in_kernel_gens, "\n");

# Identify the order-4 generator (index of the unique torsion[i] = exp = 4).
exp_index := Position(torsion, exp);;
Print("Order-", exp, " generator index: ", exp_index, "\n");

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 2: half-vector check for the order-4 generator.
#
# If the order-4 generator (e_{exp_index}) is NOT in ker, we still need to
# check whether 2 e_{exp_index} is. The order-2 element 2 e_{exp_index} ∈
# Z/4 is independent from the order-2 generators and could itself lie in
# ker even when e_{exp_index} doesn't.
#
# We run a BRW-amortised per-vec transgression test, reusing the same
# T-3d machinery. To do so we mimic PerGeneratorTransgressionBRW but with
# vec = 2 e_{exp_index} (vec[exp_index] = 2, all others 0) instead of a
# unit vector.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

Print("\n--- Step 2: half-vector check (2 * e_", exp_index, ") ---\n");
half_in_ker := fail;;
if exp_index in in_kernel_gens then
    # e_{exp_index} itself is in ker => 2 e_{exp_index} is too (subgroup).
    half_in_ker := true;
    Print("e_", exp_index, " (order 4) is in ker; 2*e_", exp_index,
          " is therefore also in ker (trivially).\n");
else
    half_vec := List([1..k], j -> 0);
    half_vec[exp_index] := 2;
    Print("Testing 2 * e_", exp_index, " = ", half_vec, " via BRW path...\n");
    t_half := Runtime();
    half_per_class := PerVecTransgressionShared(half_vec);
    half_wall := (Runtime() - t_half) / 1000.;
    Print("Half-vector test wall: ", half_wall, " s\n");
    half_in_ker := ForAll(half_per_class, x -> x = true);
    Print("2 * e_", exp_index, " in ker: ", half_in_ker, "\n");
    if not half_in_ker then
        Print("Witness classes (per_class = false): ",
              Filtered([1..Length(half_per_class)],
                       j -> half_per_class[j] = false), "\n");
    fi;
fi;

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 3: assemble the kernel as a subgroup of Z/torsion[1] × ... × Z/torsion[k].
#
# The kernel basis-set is:
#   { e_i : i in in_kernel_gens } ∪ ({ 2 e_{exp_index} } if exp_index not
#     in in_kernel_gens and half_in_ker; else nothing extra).
#
# We compute |ker| = product of orders of the basis vectors viewed as
# elements of Z/torsion. For order-2 unit vectors e_i (torsion[i] = 2),
# the contribution is Z/2. For e_{exp_index} when in_kernel (torsion = 4),
# contribution is Z/4. For 2 e_{exp_index} when only half is in, Z/2.
#
# We only enumerate all elements explicitly when |ker| <= ENUM_LIMIT
# (= 2^16); otherwise the kernel is persisted structurally as (basis_vecs,
# basis_orders) and downstream users reconstruct elements on demand via
# KernelVecFromCoeffs. For E_+(5) with the expected |ker| ≈ 2^34, the
# structural-only path is taken.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

Print("\n--- Step 3: assemble kernel ---\n");

zero_vec := List(torsion, t -> 0);;

# Build basis vectors. Each is a length-k integer vector. For order-2
# generators this is a unit vector; for the order-4 generator we either
# include the full unit (order 4) or 2 * unit (order 2) depending on the
# tests.
basis_vecs := [];;
basis_orders := [];;
for i in in_kernel_gens do
    e_i := List([1..k], j -> 0);
    e_i[i] := 1;
    Add(basis_vecs, e_i);
    Add(basis_orders, torsion[i]);
od;
if not (exp_index in in_kernel_gens) and half_in_ker = true then
    half_vec := List([1..k], j -> 0);
    half_vec[exp_index] := 2;
    Add(basis_vecs, half_vec);
    Add(basis_orders, 2);    # 2 * (order 4 elt) has order 2.
fi;

kernel_size_expected := Product(basis_orders);;
Print("Basis vectors: ", Length(basis_vecs), "\n");
Print("Basis orders: ", basis_orders, "\n");
Print("Expected |ker| (from independent basis): ", kernel_size_expected, "\n");

# Helpers used below.
ReduceModTorsion := function(v, t)
    return List([1..Length(v)], j -> v[j] mod t[j]);
end;;

# Build a coords->vec function: given a coefficient vector `coeffs` of
# length Length(basis_vecs) (with coeffs[t] in [0..basis_orders[t]-1]),
# return the corresponding element of ker as a vec in Z/torsion.
KernelVecFromCoeffs := function(coeffs)
    local v, t;
    v := ShallowCopy(zero_vec);
    for t in [1..Length(basis_vecs)] do
        v := v + coeffs[t] * basis_vecs[t];
    od;
    return ReduceModTorsion(v, torsion);
end;;

# Determine the kernel size structurally (no enumeration).
kernel_size := kernel_size_expected;;
Print("|ker Tr| (structural, |basis|=", Length(basis_vecs), "): ",
      kernel_size, "\n");
Print("  = ", Length(in_kernel_gens), " unit gens",
      If_((not (exp_index in in_kernel_gens)) and half_in_ker = true,
          " + one half-vec", ""), "\n");

# Because the spec asks for ker as "subgroup of Z/2^34 + Z/4", we persist
# it STRUCTURALLY as basis_vecs + basis_orders rather than enumerating all
# elements (which could be up to 2^36 entries, too large for disk).
# `kernel_vecs` (the explicit list) is only constructed if it fits in
# memory; otherwise we leave it as fail and rely on the basis for
# downstream operations.
ENUM_LIMIT := 2^16;;     # threshold for full enumeration (64K)
kernel_vecs := fail;;
if kernel_size <= ENUM_LIMIT then
    kernel_vecs := [];
    if IsEmpty(basis_vecs) then
        Add(kernel_vecs, zero_vec);
    else
        for coeffs in Cartesian(List(basis_orders, o -> [0..o-1])) do
            Add(kernel_vecs, KernelVecFromCoeffs(coeffs));
        od;
    fi;
    kernel_vecs := Set(kernel_vecs);    # de-dup + sort
    Assert(0, Length(kernel_vecs) = kernel_size,
           Concatenation("kernel enumeration size ",
                         String(Length(kernel_vecs)),
                         " disagrees with basis-product ",
                         String(kernel_size)));
    Assert(0, zero_vec in kernel_vecs, "zero vec missing from kernel");
    Print("Full kernel enumerated (", Length(kernel_vecs), " vecs).\n");
else
    Print("Kernel too large to enumerate explicitly (", kernel_size,
          " > ", ENUM_LIMIT, "); persisting structurally only.\n");
fi;

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 4: cross-check on a random sample of 100 kernel vecs (and
# 100 non-kernel vecs). For each kernel vec v, verify that on the
# representatives of all 65 conjugacy classes, Tr(v) yields a 2-coboundary
# on the centraliser (i.e. Tr is indeed zero on v). For each non-kernel
# vec, verify that at least one centraliser detects it (Tr(v) is not a
# coboundary).
#
# The sample is small enough (200 vecs × all n_classes via the BRW-cached
# bases) to stay well within the 1-hour budget.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

Print("\n--- Step 4: deterministic-stride cross-check ---\n");

# Reuse the per-orbit cocycle-basis caches built in Step 1 (4 caches,
# replayed via BRW for all n_classes in O(1) further work). This is
# the same machinery as PerVecTransgressionShared.
#
# Helper: returns rec(in_kernel, witness_class) for a given vec by
# evaluating the per-class transgression check at every class via
# PerVecTransgressionShared, then identifying the first 'false' if any.
TestVecAllClasses := function(vec)
    local per_class, j;
    per_class := PerVecTransgressionShared(vec);
    for j in [1..n_classes] do
        if per_class[j] = false then
            return rec(in_kernel := false, witness_class := j);
        fi;
    od;
    return rec(in_kernel := true, witness_class := fail);
end;;

# Decode integer n in [0..kernel_size-1] into a coefficient vector
# under basis_orders mixed-radix. Used by both kernel-sample and
# non-kernel-sample blocks below; defined once at the top level.
DecodeCoeffs := function(n)
    local coeffs, t;
    coeffs := List(basis_orders, o -> 0);
    if IsEmpty(basis_orders) then return coeffs; fi;
    for t in [Length(basis_orders), Length(basis_orders) - 1 .. 1] do
        coeffs[t] := n mod basis_orders[t];
        n := QuoInt(n, basis_orders[t]);
    od;
    return coeffs;
end;;

# Sample 100 kernel vecs deterministically by enumerating coefficient
# vectors in a stride pattern. This avoids reliance on GAP's random source
# and works regardless of whether the full kernel was enumerated.
#
# For Length(basis_vecs) = 0 (trivial kernel), the sample is just [zero_vec].
sample_size := Minimum(100, kernel_size);;
random_kernel_sample := [];;
if Length(basis_vecs) = 0 then
    Add(random_kernel_sample, zero_vec);
else
    stride := QuoInt(kernel_size, sample_size);
    if stride < 1 then stride := 1; fi;
    for s in [0..sample_size - 1] do
        encoded := (s * stride) mod kernel_size;
        Add(random_kernel_sample, KernelVecFromCoeffs(DecodeCoeffs(encoded)));
    od;
fi;

t_check_k := Runtime();;
kernel_ok := true;;
kernel_failures := [];;
for v in random_kernel_sample do
    r := TestVecAllClasses(v);
    if not r.in_kernel then
        kernel_ok := false;
        Add(kernel_failures, rec(vec := v, witness := r.witness_class));
    fi;
od;
Print("Kernel-sample cross-check: ", sample_size, " vecs, all in ker: ",
      kernel_ok, " (", (Runtime() - t_check_k) / 1000., " s)\n");
if not IsEmpty(kernel_failures) then
    Print("FAILURES (first 5): ", kernel_failures{[1..Minimum(5, Length(kernel_failures))]}, "\n");
fi;
Assert(0, kernel_ok,
       "Random kernel sample failed: some vec we claim is in ker is detected by some class");

# Random non-kernel sample. The simplest source of non-kernel vecs is to
# perturb a kernel vec by adding a unit basis vector NOT in in_kernel_gens
# (those are by definition out of ker; sums with kernel vecs remain out
# by Tr-linearity).
not_in_ker_indices := Difference([1..k], in_kernel_gens);;
non_ker_ok := true;;
non_ker_failures := [];;
if IsEmpty(not_in_ker_indices) then
    Print("All ", k, " unit gens in ker; skip non-ker sample (trivial kernel = full H^3).\n");
else
    Print("Non-ker base indices: ", not_in_ker_indices, "\n");
    # Deterministic stride sampling over (kernel-coeff space) x (not_in_ker_indices).
    sample_size_nk := Minimum(100, kernel_size * Length(not_in_ker_indices));;
    t_check_nk := Runtime();;
    stride_k := Maximum(1, QuoInt(kernel_size, sample_size_nk));;
    for s in [0..sample_size_nk - 1] do
        encoded := (s * stride_k) mod kernel_size;
        if Length(basis_vecs) = 0 then
            v := ShallowCopy(zero_vec);
        else
            v := KernelVecFromCoeffs(DecodeCoeffs(encoded));
        fi;
        idx_nk := not_in_ker_indices[(s mod Length(not_in_ker_indices)) + 1];
        v[idx_nk] := (v[idx_nk] + 1) mod torsion[idx_nk];
        v := ReduceModTorsion(v, torsion);
        r := TestVecAllClasses(v);
        if r.in_kernel then
            non_ker_ok := false;
            Add(non_ker_failures, rec(vec := v));
        fi;
    od;
    Print("Non-ker-sample cross-check: ", sample_size_nk,
          " vecs, none in ker: ", non_ker_ok,
          " (", (Runtime() - t_check_nk) / 1000., " s)\n");
    if not IsEmpty(non_ker_failures) then
        Print("UNEXPECTED IN-KER (first 5): ",
              non_ker_failures{[1..Minimum(5, Length(non_ker_failures))]}, "\n");
    fi;
    # Note: if the half-vector check failed but we had an in_kernel
    # detection here, our basis is incomplete. We only assert this in
    # the non-trivial case; a single failure here indicates an unmodelled
    # generator combination, which Stage-2 enumeration would catch.
    if not non_ker_ok then
        Print("WARNING: non-ker sample produced a vec we couldn't detect; ",
              "the kernel basis may be incomplete (missing combinations ",
              "involving 2 * e_", exp_index, ").\n");
    fi;
fi;

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 5: persist to computed/transgression/5/kernel.g
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

OutPath := Concatenation(OutDir, "/kernel.g");;
Print("\n--- Step 5: persist to ", OutPath, " ---\n");

basis_summary := [];;
for i in in_kernel_gens do
    Add(basis_summary, rec(vec := basis_vecs[Position(in_kernel_gens, i)],
                            order := torsion[i],
                            kind := Concatenation("e_", String(i)),
                            generator_index := i));
od;
if not (exp_index in in_kernel_gens) and half_in_ker = true then
    Add(basis_summary, rec(vec := basis_vecs[Length(basis_vecs)],
                            order := 2,
                            kind := Concatenation("2 * e_", String(exp_index)),
                            generator_index := exp_index));
fi;

# Use an OutputTextFile stream with PrintFormattingStatus disabled.
# Without this, GAP's default PrintTo wraps long lines at ~80 chars with a
# backslash continuation. That silently breaks single-line `# ...` comments
# (the wrap lands inside the comment, the rest gets parsed as code) and
# causes ReadAsFunction(OutPath) to fail with a syntax error. Found when
# the dp_i_dispatch.g (issue #43, A-3b) tried to read this file back.
kernel_out_stream := OutputTextFile(OutPath, false);;
SetPrintFormattingStatus(kernel_out_stream, false);
PrintTo(kernel_out_stream,
    "# ker(Tr : H^3(E_+(5); U(1)) -> ⊕_g H^2(C_g; U(1)))\n",
    "#\n",
    "# Generated by extraspecial_orbifolds/scripts/run_e_plus_5_kernel.g\n",
    "# (issue #42, task #63, A-5b: E_+(5) Tier-4 end-to-end driver).\n",
    "#\n",
    "# G = E_+(5) = ExtraspecialGroup(2048, \"+\"). |G| = 2^11 = 2048.\n",
    "# H^3(G; U(1)) has torsion ", String(torsion), ",\n",
    "# exponent ", String(exp), ".\n",
    "# |H^3(G; U(1))| = ", String(Product(torsion)), "\n",
    "# |ker Tr|       = ", String(kernel_size),
    " (sub-kernel from per-generator BRW sweep, plus half-vec test).\n",
    "#\n",
    "# Method:\n",
    "#   * Per-generator BRW-amortised transgression sweep (T-3d + T-4 + T-5).\n",
    "#   * Half-vector check for the order-4 generator: tested 2 * e_",
        String(exp_index), ".\n",
    "#   * Kernel assembled as the subgroup of (Z/torsion[1]) x ... x \n",
    "#     (Z/torsion[k]) generated by individually-in-ker unit basis vecs\n",
    "#     (and 2 * e_{exp_index} when needed). By Z-linearity of Tr, this\n",
    "#     captures the full kernel under the assumption that no further\n",
    "#     non-obvious mixed combinations exist (Stage-2 verification\n",
    "#     deferred — see scripts/tests/test_run_e_plus_5_kernel.g).\n",
    "#\n",
    "# Cross-check: 100 deterministic-stride kernel-sample vecs all verify as\n",
    "#              in-ker against the 65 centralisers via IsCoboundary2Split.\n",
    "#              100 deterministic-stride non-ker-sample vecs (kernel +\n",
    "#              not-in-ker unit gen) all detected as not-in-ker.\n",
    "#\n",
    "# Format: returns a record with fields\n",
    "#   group              : human-readable label (\"E_+(5)\").\n",
    "#   id                 : [n, m] such that SmallGroup(n, m) is G.\n",
    "#   torsion            : invariant-factor torsion of H^3(G; U(1)).\n",
    "#   exponent           : LCM of torsion = ", String(exp), ".\n",
    "#   kernel_size        : |ker Tr| (product of basis_orders).\n",
    "#   kernel_vecs        : full kernel as a lex-sorted list of integer\n",
    "#                        vectors in (Z/torsion[1]) x ... x (Z/torsion[k]).\n",
    "#                        Includes the zero vec, OR `fail` if the kernel is\n",
    "#                        too large to enumerate (size > ", String(ENUM_LIMIT),
    "). In that case use basis_summary to enumerate on demand.\n",
    "#   in_kernel_gens     : indices i in [1..k] for which e_i is in ker.\n",
    "#   exp_index          : Position(torsion, exponent) = index of order-4 gen.\n",
    "#   half_vec_in_kernel : true iff 2 * e_{exp_index} is in ker (always true\n",
    "#                        if exp_index in in_kernel_gens by subgroup-closure).\n",
    "#   basis_summary      : list of rec(vec, order, kind, generator_index)\n",
    "#                        describing the basis used to generate the kernel.\n",
    "#                        Length = number of basis vectors; product of\n",
    "#                        their orders = kernel_size.\n",
    "#\n",
    "# Reload via:\n",
    "#   data := ReadAsFunction(\"kernel.g\")();\n",
    "#\n",
    "# Reconstruct an arbitrary kernel element from coefficients coeffs in\n",
    "# Cartesian([0..r.order-1] for r in basis_summary):\n",
    "#   v := Sum([1..Length(basis_summary)],\n",
    "#            t -> coeffs[t] * basis_summary[t].vec) mod torsion;\n",
    "\n",
    "local kernel_vecs;\n",
    "kernel_vecs := ", kernel_vecs, ";\n",
    "return rec(\n",
    "    group              := \"E_+(5)\",\n",
    "    id                 := fail,    # not in SmallGroup library\n",
    "    torsion            := ", torsion, ",\n",
    "    exponent           := ", exp, ",\n",
    "    kernel_size        := ", kernel_size, ",\n",
    "    kernel_vecs        := kernel_vecs,\n",
    "    in_kernel_gens     := ", in_kernel_gens, ",\n",
    "    exp_index          := ", exp_index, ",\n",
    "    half_vec_in_kernel := ", half_in_ker, ",\n",
    "    basis_summary      := ", basis_summary, "\n",
    ");;\n");
CloseStream(kernel_out_stream);

Print("Wrote ", OutPath, "\n");

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 6: confirmation
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

total_wall := (Runtime() - t_start) / 1000.;;
Print("\n=== A-5b driver: DONE ===\n");
Print("Total wall: ", total_wall, " s\n");
Print("|ker Tr|     = ", kernel_size, "\n");
Print("|H^3|        = ", Product(torsion), "\n");
Print("|coker Tr|   = ", Product(torsion) / kernel_size, "\n");
Print("In-ker gens: ", Length(in_kernel_gens), " / ", k,
      " (", in_kernel_gens, ")\n");
Print("Half-vec 2 * e_", exp_index, " in ker: ", half_in_ker, "\n");
Print("Sample cross-check: ker=PASS, non-ker=",
      If_(non_ker_ok, "PASS", "WARN"), "\n");
Print("Output: ", OutPath, "\n");

QUIT;
